leap71/PicoGKRuntime @ 0f26321c18ed (26.2): pass the mesh to meshToLevelSet
without copying it.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of PicoGK (not affiliated with or endorsed by LEAP 71), applies the same change
to its vendored runtime as patches/PicoGKRuntime/0005-render-mesh-zero-copy.patch.
That file sits on top of picovoxel's patches 0001-0004, which shift
PicoGKVdbVoxels.h by about 200 lines; this file is the same change against
pristine 0f26321c18ed, with only the hunk offset differing.

The change. Voxels::RenderMesh builds its grid through roFloatGridFromMesh,
which copies every vertex and triangle of the Mesh one at a time
(GetVertex/GetTriangle) into two fresh std::vectors that grow by push_back
with no reserve, and then calls openvdb::tools::meshToLevelSet. The copy is
serial and runs before the parallel mesher starts. Mesh already stores what the
mesher reads: std::vector<Vector3> is three packed floats per vertex, the layout
of openvdb::Vec3s, and std::vector<Triangle> is three 32-bit indices per
triangle, the layout of openvdb::Vec3I. The patch passes the two vectors to
meshToLevelSet directly, the way the dilated-mesh Voxels constructor in the same
file already does, and adds two static_asserts on the layouts. meshToLevelSet's
QuadAndTriangleDataAdapter then reads the triangles in place; its own parallel
world-to-index transform is the only remaining pass over the vertices.

Output values: unchanged. The same openvdb function receives the same element
values in the same order (vertex indices are never negative, so the int32 to
uint32 view reads the same numbers). picovoxel's pinned grid hashes and example
outputs did not move.

Measured: see the U26 row of picovoxel's upstream/LEDGER.md. The saving scales
with the element count and applies to every RenderMesh caller, including
Voxels::CalculateProperties, which re-voxelizes a mesh.

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree.
Not applied by picovoxel's build; see upstream/README.md.

--- a/Source/PicoGKVdbVoxels.h
+++ b/Source/PicoGKVdbVoxels.h
@@ -830,33 +830,20 @@
                                                 VoxelSize oVoxelSize,
                                                 int32_t nHalfWidth)
     {
-        std::vector< openvdb::Vec3s >   oVertices;
-        std::vector< openvdb::Vec3I >   oTriangles;
-        
-        for (int32_t n=0; n<oMesh.nVertexCount(); n++)
-        {
-            Vector3 v(0.0f,0.0f,0.0f);
-            oMesh.GetVertex(n, (Vector3*)&v);
-            oVertices.push_back(openvdb::Vec3s( v.X,
-                                                v.Y,
-                                                v.Z));
-        }
-        
-        for (int32_t n=0; n<oMesh.nTriangleCount(); n++)
-        {
-            Triangle t;
-            oMesh.GetTriangle(n, &t);
-            oTriangles.push_back(openvdb::Vec3I(    (unsigned int) t.A,
-                                                    (unsigned int) t.B,
-                                                    (unsigned int) t.C));
-        }
+        // Hand the mesh's own storage to the mesher instead of copying it
+        // element by element into fresh, unreserved vectors. Vector3 and
+        // Vec3s are both three packed floats, and Triangle and Vec3I both
+        // three 32-bit indices, so the mesher reads the same values; the
+        // dilated-mesh constructor above already relies on this layout.
+        static_assert(sizeof(Vector3)  == sizeof(openvdb::Vec3s), "Vector3 must match Vec3s");
+        static_assert(sizeof(Triangle) == sizeof(openvdb::Vec3I), "Triangle must match Vec3I");
 
         openvdb::math::Transform::Ptr roTransform
             = openvdb::math::Transform::createLinearTransform(oVoxelSize);
 
         return openvdb::tools::meshToLevelSet<openvdb::FloatGrid>(  *roTransform,
-                                                                    oVertices,
-                                                                    oTriangles,
+                                                                    (const std::vector<openvdb::Vec3s>&) oMesh.vVertices(),
+                                                                    (const std::vector<openvdb::Vec3I>&) oMesh.vTriangles(),
                                                                     nHalfWidth);
     }
     
